or(\\\"Each distance entry must have exactly 3 elements\\\")\\n        u, v, d = dist_entry\\n        if u not in node_set:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Unknown node '{u}' in distances\\\")\\n        if v not in node_set:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Unknown node '{v}' in distances\\\")\\n        if u == v:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Self-loop detected for node '{u}'\\\")\\n\\n        canon = (min(u, v), max(u, v))\\n        if canon in seen_pairs:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Duplicate edge for pair {canon}\\\")\\n        seen_pairs.add(canon)\\n\\n        mr = max(core_distance[canon[0]], core_distance[canon[1]], d)\\n        mr_edges.append((mr, canon[0], canon[1]))\\n\\n    mr_edges.sort(key=lambda e: (e[0], e[1], e[2]))\\n\\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\\n\\n    def find(x):\\n        root = x\\n        while parent[root] != root:\\n            root = parent[root]\\n        curr = x\\n        while curr != root:\\n            nxt = parent[curr]\\n            parent[curr] = root\\n            curr = nxt\\n        return root\\n\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] \u003c rank[ry]:\\n            rx, ry = ry, rx\\n        parent[ry] = rx\\n        if rank[rx] == rank[ry]:\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n\\n    selected_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n\\n    for mr, u, v in mr_edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            selected_edges.append({\\\"u\\\": u, \\\"v\\\": v, \\\"weight\\\": mr})\\n            total_weight += mr\\n            if len(selected_edges) == n - 1:\\n                break\\n\\n    if len(selected_edges) != n - 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Input graph is disconnected; MST cannot be formed\\\")\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"mst_edges\\\": selected_edges,\\n        \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight\\n    }\"}\n\nLet me double-check the f-strings. In Python 3, f\"Missing required field: '{field}'\" has single quotes inside. When JSON-encoding, the double quotes in the f-string need to be escaped, but single quotes don't. The curly braces in f-strings are fine since they're the variable references.\n\nWait, actually in f-strings the variable is wrapped in {} which is not an issue in JSON (no need to escape). The issue is just the double quotes.\n\nLet me be careful about the f-strings with nested quotes:\n- `f\"Missing required field: '{field}'\"` → in JSON: `f\\\"Missing required field: '{field}'\\\"`\n- `f\"Missing core distance for node '{node}'\"` → `f\\\"Missing core distance for node '{node}'\\\"`\n- etc.\n\nAlso the dict comprehensions use `node: node` which has no quotes.\n\nAnd `{\"u\": u, \"v\": v, \"weight\": mr}` → `{\\\"u\\\": u, \\\"v\\\": v, \\\"weight\\\": mr}`\n\nAnd `{\"mst_edges\": selected_edges, \"total_weight\": total_weight}` → `{\\\"mst_edges\\\": selected_edges, \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight}`\n\nI also need to check: `raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate edge for pair {canon}\")` - this has `{canon}` inside the f-string which is fine for JSON encoding.\n\nLet me also reconsider whether I should include both edges using original identifier vs canonicalized. The task says \"Canonicalize undirected endpoints\" so I should use the canonical form in the output too.\n\nOK, let me produce the final JSON output. I'll be careful to properly escape all quotes and newlines.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    required = [\\\"nodes\\\", \\\"core_distance\\\", \\\"distances\\\"]\\n    for field in required:\\n        if field not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing required field: '{field}'\\\")\\n\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    core_distance = case[\\\"core_distance\\\"]\\n    distances = case[\\\"distances\\\"]\\n\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or len(nodes) == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"'nodes' must be a 